{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Well, let's start with a few important items:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query is a "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"server-state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" library, responsible for managing asynchronous operations between your server and client"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Vuex, Pinia, Zustand, etc. are "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"client-state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" libraries that "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"can be used to store asynchronous data, albeit inefficiently when compared to a tool like TanStack Query"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With those points in mind, the short answer is that TanStack Query "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"replaces the boilerplate code and related wiring used to manage cache data in your client-state and replaces it with just a few lines of code."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For a vast majority of applications, the truly "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"globally accessible client state"}]},{"type":"text","value":" that is left over after migrating all of your async code to TanStack Query is usually very tiny."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are still some circumstances where an application might indeed have a massive amount of synchronous client-only state (like a visual designer or music production application), in which case, you will probably still want a client state manager. In this situation it's important to note that "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Query is not a replacement for local/client state management"}]},{"type":"text","value":". However, you can use TanStack Query alongside most client state managers with zero issues."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"a-contrived-example","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A Contrived Example"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here we have some \"global\" state being managed by a global state library:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const globalState = {\n  projects,\n  teams,\n  tasks,\n  users,\n  themeMode,\n  sidebarStatus,\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Currently, the global state manager is caching 4 types of server-state: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"projects"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"teams"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tasks"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"users"},{"type":"text","value":". If we were to move these server-state assets to TanStack Query, our remaining global state would look more like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const globalState = {\n  themeMode,\n  sidebarStatus,\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This also means that with a few hook calls to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useQuery"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useMutation"},{"type":"text","value":", we also get to remove any boilerplate code that was used to manage our server state e.g."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Connectors"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Action Creators"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Middlewares"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reducers"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Loading/Error/Result states"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contexts"}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"With all of those things removed, you may ask yourself, "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"Is it worth it to keep using our client state manager for this tiny global state?\""}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And that's up to you!"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"But TanStack Query's role is clear. It removes asynchronous wiring and boilerplate from your application and replaces it with just a few lines of code."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What are you waiting for, give it a go already!"}]}],"headings":[{"id":"a-contrived-example","text":"A Contrived Example","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nWell, let's start with a few important items:\n\n- TanStack Query is a **server-state** library, responsible for managing asynchronous operations between your server and client\n- Vuex, Pinia, Zustand, etc. are **client-state** libraries that _can be used to store asynchronous data, albeit inefficiently when compared to a tool like TanStack Query_\n\nWith those points in mind, the short answer is that TanStack Query **replaces the boilerplate code and related wiring used to manage cache data in your client-state and replaces it with just a few lines of code.**\n\nFor a vast majority of applications, the truly **globally accessible client state** that is left over after migrating all of your async code to TanStack Query is usually very tiny.\n\n> There are still some circumstances where an application might indeed have a massive amount of synchronous client-only state (like a visual designer or music production application), in which case, you will probably still want a client state manager. In this situation it's important to note that **TanStack Query is not a replacement for local/client state management**. However, you can use TanStack Query alongside most client state managers with zero issues.\n\n## A Contrived Example\n\nHere we have some \"global\" state being managed by a global state library:\n\n```tsx\nconst globalState = {\n  projects,\n  teams,\n  tasks,\n  users,\n  themeMode,\n  sidebarStatus,\n}\n```\n\nCurrently, the global state manager is caching 4 types of server-state: `projects`, `teams`, `tasks`, and `users`. If we were to move these server-state assets to TanStack Query, our remaining global state would look more like this:\n\n```tsx\nconst globalState = {\n  themeMode,\n  sidebarStatus,\n}\n```\n\nThis also means that with a few hook calls to `useQuery` and `useMutation`, we also get to remove any boilerplate code that was used to manage our server state e.g.\n\n- Connectors\n- Action Creators\n- Middlewares\n- Reducers\n- Loading/Error/Result states\n- Contexts\n\nWith all of those things removed, you may ask yourself, **\"Is it worth it to keep using our client state manager for this tiny global state?\"**\n\n**And that's up to you!**\n\nBut TanStack Query's role is clear. It removes asynchronous wiring and boilerplate from your application and replaces it with just a few lines of code.\n\nWhat are you waiting for, give it a go already!\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: does-this-replace-client-state\ntitle: Does Vue Query replace Vuex, Pinia or other global state managers?\nref: docs/framework/react/guides/does-this-replace-client-state.md\nreplace: { 'Redux, MobX': 'Vuex, Pinia' }\n---\n"}
